<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="Math234Sp19-Midterm1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Math 234 - Midterm 1 - Spring 2019</title>
	<exercise>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the equation of the line through the point <m>(1,2,3)</m> with direction vector <m>\langle 4,0,-2\rangle</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The equation of the line in vector form is:
					<me>\vec{r}(t) = \langle 1, 2, 3 \rangle + t \langle 4, 0, -2 \rangle.</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use traces to sketch the surface <m>z=x^2+9 y^2</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Note: Your 3D sketch can be a rough sketch, as long as the general shape is recognizable. Your trace sketches, though, should be clear.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write down an integral that represents the length of the curve <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle 2 t, \sin (t), e^t\rangle</m> from the point <m>(0,0,1)</m> to the point <m>(2 \pi, 0, e^\pi)</m>. You do NOT need to compute the integral.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The point <m>(0,0,1)</m> corresponds to <m>t=0</m> and <m>(2\pi, 0, e^\pi)</m> corresponds to <m>t=\pi</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}'(t) \amp = \langle 2, \cos t, e^t \rangle \\
						|\vec{r}'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{4 + \cos^2 t + e^{2t}} \\
						L \amp = \int_0^\pi \sqrt{4 + \cos^2 t + e^{2t}} \, dt
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Show that if <m>|\vec{r}(t)|=4</m>, then <m>\vec{r}'(t)</m> is orthogonal to <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>. (Hint: recall that <m>|\vec{r}(t)|^2=\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t)</m>).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Since <m>|\vec{r}(t)| = 4</m>, we have <m>\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t) = 16</m>.
					Differentiating both sides:
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\frac{d}{dt} (\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t)) \amp = \frac{d}{dt} (16) \\
						\vec{r}'(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t) + \vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{r}'(t) \amp = 0 \\
						2 \vec{r}'(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t) \amp = 0 \\
						\vec{r}'(t) \cdot \vec{r}(t) \amp = 0
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
					Thus, <m>\vec{r}'(t)</m> is orthogonal to <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				An astronaut travels to the purple planet, where the acceleration due to gravity is <m>4 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}</m>. He kicks a soccer ball at a speed of <m>14 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}</m> at an angle of 30 degrees (the ball is on the ground when he kicks it). Note: <m>\cos \left(30^{\circ}\right)=\sqrt{3} / 2</m> and <m>\sin \left(30^{\circ}\right)=1 / 2</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write vector-valued functions to describe the velocity and position of the soccer ball as it flies.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The acceleration is <m>\vec{a}(t) = \langle 0, -4 \rangle</m>.
					The initial velocity is <m>\vec{v}(0) = \langle 14 \cos 30^\circ, 14 \sin 30^\circ \rangle = \langle 7\sqrt{3}, 7 \rangle</m>.
					The initial position is <m>\vec{r}(0) = \langle 0, 0 \rangle</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{v}(t) \amp = \int \vec{a}(t) \, dt = \langle 7\sqrt{3}, 7 - 4t \rangle \\
						\vec{r}(t) \amp = \int \vec{v}(t) \, dt = \langle 7\sqrt{3}t, 7t - 2t^2 \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					At what time does it land?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The ball lands when the vertical component of the position is zero:
					<me>7t - 2t^2 = 0 \implies t(7 - 2t) = 0.</me>
					The ball lands at <m>t = 3.5</m> seconds.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find and sketch the domain of the function
				<me>f(x, y)=\frac{\sqrt{y-x^2}}{x^2+4}</me>
			</p>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<p>
				We need <m>y - x^2 \ge 0 \implies y \ge x^2</m>. The denominator <m>x^2 + 4</m> is never zero.
				The domain is <m>\{(x, y) \mid y \ge x^2\}</m>, which is the region on and above the parabola <m>y = x^2</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Match the graph that corresponds to each of the following functions.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(x, y)=\sqrt{x^2+y^2-4}</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Graph 2.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(x, y)=y</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Graph 1.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(x, y)=|x-y|</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Graph 5.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(x, y)=\frac{1}{x^2+y^2}</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Graph 6.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(x, y)=\ln \left(x^2+y^2-1\right)</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Graph 3.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<m>f(x, y)=9-x^2-y^2</m>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Graph 4.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Consider the curve <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle t, 2 \sin (t), 2 \cos (t)\rangle</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute the tangential, normal and binormal vectors when <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>. That is, <m>\vec{T}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right), \vec{N}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> and <m>\vec{B}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\vec{r}'(t) \amp = \langle 1, 2 \cos t, -2 \sin t \rangle \\
						|\vec{r}'(t)| \amp = \sqrt{1 + 4 \cos^2 t + 4 \sin^2 t} = \sqrt{5} \\
						\vec{T}(t) \amp = \langle \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}, \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \cos t, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \sin t \rangle \implies \vec{T}(\frac{\pi}{2}) = \langle \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}, 0, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \rangle \\
						\vec{T}'(t) \amp = \langle 0, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \sin t, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \cos t \rangle \implies \vec{T}\,'(\frac{\pi}{2}) = \langle 0, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}, 0 \rangle \\
						|\vec{T}\,'(\frac{\pi}{2})| \amp = \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}} \\
						\vec{N}(\frac{\pi}{2}) \amp = \langle 0, -1, 0 \rangle \\
						\vec{B}(\frac{\pi}{2}) \amp = \vec{T}(\frac{\pi}{2}) \times \vec{N}(\frac{\pi}{2}) = \begin{vmatrix} \hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\ 1/\sqrt{5} \amp 0 \amp -2/\sqrt{5} \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \end{vmatrix} = \langle -\frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}, 0, -\frac{1}{\sqrt{5}} \rangle
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compute the curvature <m>\kappa</m> when <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\kappa(\frac{\pi}{2}) = \frac{|\vec{T}\,'(\frac{\pi}{2})|}{|\vec{r}\,'(\frac{\pi}{2})|} = \frac{2/\sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{5}} = \frac{2}{5}.
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the equations of the osculating plane, normal plane and rectifying plane when <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The point is <m>\vec{r}(\frac{\pi}{2}) = \langle \frac{\pi}{2}, 2, 0 \rangle</m>.
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\text{Osculating Plane: } \amp - \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}(x - \frac{\pi}{2}) - \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(z - 0) = 0 \implies 2x + z = \pi \\
						\text{Normal Plane: } \amp \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(x - \frac{\pi}{2}) - \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}(z - 0) = 0 \implies x - 2z = \frac{\pi}{2} \\
						\text{Rectifying Plane: } \amp -1(y - 2) = 0 \implies y = 2
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
